# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever cloned the repository.
#
# Without this file, a Windows clone with git's default core.autocrlf=true
# converts every text file to CRLF on checkout -- including the shell scripts.
# A shell script whose shebang ends in a carriage return cannot be run at all:
# Linux looks for an interpreter literally named "/bin/sh\r", does not find
# one, and reports
#
#     bash: ./tools/check-environment.sh: cannot execute: required file not found
#
# which names neither the script nor the line endings, and reads as the course
# being broken on the first command it asks a learner to type inside the
# container.
#
# What git stores has always been LF; the conversion happens on checkout, so
# the repository looks correct to anyone inspecting it on Linux or macOS and
# only Windows learners ever see it. That is the worst shape for a defect on
# this course, because the people who would notice are not the people it
# happens to.
#
# tests/sweep.py refuses a CR in any of these.

# Everything is LF in the repository and LF on disk, whatever the platform.
* text=auto eol=lf

# ...except PowerShell, which is only ever read on Windows and is conventionally
# CRLF there. Nothing in Linux parses these.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Declared rather than left to git's guessing, because a file it guesses wrong
# about is a file it corrupts. None of these are tracked today; they are listed
# so that adding one later is safe.
*.elf   binary
*.bin   binary
*.hex   binary
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.pdf   binary
*.xlsx  binary
*.zip   binary
